00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:07.640
Elsewhere in the course, we saw the pass keyword&nbsp;
and its use as a placeholder within code. We used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:13.520
pass within the context of a loop. Now, we&nbsp;
want to know how pass is used in the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:20.760
of a function. We also want to see what an output&nbsp;
screen shows when pass is used within a function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:28.680
The eventual goal of this code example, 425-pass,&nbsp;
is to calculate the total amount for a car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:35.120
given its amount, the licensing fee,&nbsp;
and all applicable taxes. As of now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.960
we do not know what the proper formula is for&nbsp;
calculating the total car amount. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:50.680
on lines 1 and 2, we have the calculate total&nbsp;
function with the pass keyword. In this context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:56.720
the pass keyword is being used as a placeholder&nbsp;
for future code. We can wait for the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.880
or calculations needed to finish creating the&nbsp;
calculate_total function. The get amounts function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:14.400
starts on line 4 and, on lines 5 and 6, gathers&nbsp;
input for a car amount and a license amount and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:24.160
converts those entries to integers so that they&nbsp;
can be used in calculations. Then, on line 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:32.280
the total is rendered in a print statement. The&nbsp;
amount is printed on line 8. And the get_amounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:40.840
function is run and stored in a subtotal variable&nbsp;
on line 10. I will run this code and enter the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:50.000
dollar amounts called for in the input statements.&nbsp;
Notice the result. The print statement runs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.680
but the total does not show because the only&nbsp;
code in the calculate_total function is in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.000
pass statement. The benefit of having the pass&nbsp;
statement here is that, within this code, we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.880
still test the functionality of the get_amounts&nbsp;
function. At the same time, we await the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:15.000
information needed to finish the calculate_total&nbsp;
function, which will save time for later testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:23.000
of this code, as we now know one function works as&nbsp;
expected. We have come to the end of the section&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.040
on functions. That means it’s time to analyze a&nbsp;
block of code and answer some questions about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:33.520
code. Look over the 425-analyze code and then&nbsp;
answer the questions in the workbook project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.640
Review 4.2. I encourage you to enter this code&nbsp;
into a code editor and then run the code to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:46.440
answer the questions in the review exercise. We&nbsp;
will go through the answers in the next video.

